{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012eRxJ94nN2DZ96KBsd8U2P","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully and propose the next task.\n\n## Step 1: Analyze Project Context\n\nThis is a Go project that reimplements Unix utilities from specifications derived from C source code. The pipeline is: extract specs from C source → write PRDs → generate Go code → verify via differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n## Step 2: Summarize Project State\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. Re-implementations built from documentation alone diverge in observable behavior. This project derives specs from C source and uses AI code generation to produce Go implementations, verified by differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**:\n- `cmd/` - One package per utility (ts, wc, cat, sponge, ls, du, etc.)\n\n\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - Syscall abstractions (terminal width, file metadata, signals)\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting (columns, ANSI color, human-readable sizes)\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n- `docs/` - Specifications (PRDs, use cases, test suites)\n\n**Current state of implementation**: `pkg/testutils` and `cmd/ts` are already implemented with their test suites in place.\n\n**Current release**: rel01.0 (in_progress) - \"Batch 1 — ts specification and differential test\". Looking at the actual source code, the implementation appears complete—`cmd/ts/main.go`, `cmd/ts/ts_test.go`, `pkg/testutils/difftest.go`, and `pkg/testutils/normalize.go` all exist with full implementations. The use case status shows \"in_progress\" but the code itself seems finished, so I need to double-check what's actually still pending.\n\nThe documentation says the PRD is complete, use case is written, test suite is written, but implementation is pending—yet the source code files are there. This suggests either the docs are outdated or the code was generated after the status was recorded. Since the code exists for ts and testutils, the next logical step is to verify everything works or move forward to rel01.1, which needs wc, cat, and sponge implementations.\n\nFor rel01.1, all three commands have their PRDs, use cases, and test suites ready but no implementation yet. They only depend on testutils which already exists. Following the dependency ordering principle, I should implement them in sequence: wc first with its tests, then cat with its tests, then sponge with its tests.\n\nNow I'm checking whether rel01.0 is actually complete—the ts source code exists, but the status still shows it as in_progress. Since the code is already there, I should probably move forward with proposing the first task for rel01.1, which would be implementing cmd/cat since it comes first alphabetically among the remaining utilities. cmd/cat comes first alphabetically, so that's the next task. I'm focusing on the earliest incomplete release per the priority rules, and since all three utilities in rel01.1 are independent and only depend on existing code, I can start with cmd/cat implementation. Let me check the line budget against the requirements to see if this is feasible.\n\nLooking at the five requirement categories for cat, I'm estimating the implementation should land around 250-350 lines once I account for flag parsing, the various display modes, line numbering, blank squeezing, and error handling including SIGPIPE. That's manageable as a single task if I keep it to implementation only and defer the tests.\n\nNow I'm checking against the guidelines for code tasks—they should target 5-8 requirements and acceptance criteria. With 25 total items across R1-R5, that's too granular. I think the right approach is to group by the five requirement categories themselves rather than individual items, so the task requirements map cleanly to R1 through R5.\n\nThat gives me six requirements total (including SIGPIPE handling), which fits the target range. For acceptance criteria, I'm outlining the key validation points: the code compiles cleanly, basic passthrough works, and then specific behaviors for each feature flag.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the design approach—using bufio.Scanner for line-by-line processing with transformation flags, applying transformations in a specific order (squeeze first, then non-printing characters, then line endings, then numbering), and handling SIGPIPE with a signal handler that exits cleanly.\n\nFor flag parsing, I need to decide between Go's standard flag package and manual parsing. Since cat only uses short flags according to the PRD, manual parsing might be simpler to handle combined flags like -vET properly, though the standard package could work too.\n\nThe core design decisions are clear: apply transformations in order (squeeze blanks, then non-printing characters, then end-of-line markers, then line numbers), handle flag equivalences like -A being -vET, treat -u as a no-op, and handle SIGPIPE correctly.\n\nFor the implementation, I'll create cmd/cat/main.go as the single file needed for this utility, following the same structure as the existing ts command.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the release status—rel01.0 is marked in_progress, so maybe I should verify the ts implementation is complete first before moving to cat. Let me check what the success criteria actually require: the ts command needs to compile, handle timestamped output, pass differential tests for default and special modes, handle empty input correctly, and pass the test suite.\n\nSince the code already exists and I can't run tests directly, I'll assume it's working and move forward with implementing cmd/cat per prd006-cat R1-R5. I need to be careful about the existing patterns though—I should reference how cmd/ts is structured and make sure the stitch agent understands the pkg/testutils package that's already in place, particularly the difftest utilities.\n\nFor this implementation task, the stitch agent will need the PRD itself, the architecture documentation to understand the project structure, the test suite that will validate the work, and cmd/ts as a reference pattern.\n\nGenerating task definition... Looking at the requirements for line numbering, blank-line squeezing, and non-printing character display — these are the core features I need to implement alongside the default passthrough behavior, with specific formatting rules for each flag combination.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the error handling requirements and flag parsing strategy: I need to handle file open errors and stdout write errors gracefully while continuing to process remaining files, support both individual flags and combined forms like -vET, and recognize '-' as stdin. The transformation order matters too — squeezing blanks first, then applying non-printing display, then end-of-line markers. of-line marker (-E), then line number prefix (-n/-b).\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use the same SIGPIPE handling pattern as cmd/ts: install a goroutine-based signal handler that calls os.Exit(0) on SIGPIPE, per prd006-cat R5.4.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"For default mode (no transformation flags), use io.Copy for efficient binary passthrough per prd006-cat R1.4. When any transformation flag is active, switch to byte-by-byte or line-by-line processing.\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"Use manual flag parsing (not the flag package) to support combined short flags like -vET matching GNU cat's getopt behavior. Follow the same manual parsing pattern used in cmd/ts/main.go.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/cat compiles with 'go build ./cmd/cat/...' with no errors\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"go run ./cmd/cat with no flags and text stdin produces verbatim output (no added/removed bytes)\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"go run ./cmd/cat -n produces lines prefixed with right-justified 6-wide numbers and tab separator\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"go run ./cmd/cat -b numbers only non-blank lines; blank lines have no number prefix\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"go run ./cmd/cat -s collapses three consecutive blank lines to one\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"go run ./cmd/cat -v on input byte 0x01 produces '^A',\"\n\nI'm continuing to review the acceptance criteria for the cat command implementation. The -v flag should render control characters and high bytes in a specific format, the -A flag combines multiple visualization modes, and error handling needs to gracefully skip missing files while still processing valid ones.\n\nNow I'm checking the task ordering against the canonical sequence—documentation, types, libraries, implementations, then tests. Since all PRDs and test suites are already written for this release, the cat implementation is the right next task to tackle.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":85993,"cache_read_input_tokens":18565,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":85993},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"89b02b81-8cd4-47f3-a924-f7655a560be9","uuid":"67ed64e3-a913-4258-8ac4-c015a1ce4130"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012eRxJ94nN2DZ96KBsd8U2P","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specs. This project derives specifications from C source, generates Go implementations via AI, and verifies parity against GNU reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**: `cmd/<utility>/` per-utility packages, `pkg/testutils/` differential testing harness, `pkg/sys/` syscall abstractions, `pkg/format/` output formatting, `magefiles/` build targets, `docs/` specification suite.\n\n**Current state**: `pkg/testutils` and `cmd/ts` are implemented (source code exists). All other utilities have PRDs and test suites written but no code. rel01.0 (ts) has code in place. rel01.1 (wc, cat, sponge) is pending -- all three have complete PRDs, use cases, and test suites but no implementation.\n\n**Current release**: rel01.0 is in-progress but code exists; rel01.1 is the next target with three utilities: wc, cat, sponge. None require `pkg/sys` or `pkg/format` -- only `pkg/testutils` which is implemented.\n\n## Priority Analysis\n\nrel01.0 has code implemented. The next incomplete release is rel01.1 with three independent utilities. Per canonical ordering (P8), `cmd/cat` comes first alphabetically. Per P2, implementation and tests are separate tasks. The first task is the `cmd/cat` implementation.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Implement cmd/cat concatenation and display (prd006-cat R1-R5)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd006-cat.yaml (full PRD for cat -- all requirements R1 through R5)\n      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel01.1.yaml (test cases this implementation must satisfy -- cat section)\n      - cmd/ts/main.go (pattern reference for utility structure, manual flag parsing, SIGPIPE handling)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (DiffTest struct and RunDiffTests function used by the test file)\n      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (project structure, component responsibilities, naming conventions)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/cat/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"Complete cat implementation: concatenation, line numbering (-n/-b), blank squeezing (-s), non-printing display (-v/-E/-T/-A/-e/-t/-u), error handling, SIGPIPE\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement default passthrough behavior per prd006-cat R1.1-R1.5: read named files in argument order or stdin when no files given, write content verbatim to stdout, treat '-' as stdin, concatenate multiple files with no separator, preserve binary data without corruption, do not add or remove newlines.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement line numbering per prd006-cat R2.1-R2.4: -n prepends right-justified width-6 line number followed by tab to every line (numbering starts at 1, does not reset per file). -b numbers only non-blank lines (blank lines get no number and no tab). -b overrides -n when both given. A blank line is one containing only a newline character.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement blank-line squeezing per prd006-cat R3.1-R3.3: -s suppresses repeated blank lines (first blank is kept, subsequent consecutive blanks discarded). Squeezing applies across file boundaries. -s is compatible with -n and -b -- squeezing is applied before numbering so suppressed lines do not consume line numbers.\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement non-printing display per prd006-cat R4.1-R4.9: -v shows control chars 0x00-0x1F (except 0x09 tab, 0x0A newline) as ^X, 0x7F as ^?, 0x80-0x9F as M-^X, 0xA0-0xFE as M-X, 0xFF as M-^?. -E appends '$' before each newline. -T shows tab as ^I. -A equals -vET. -e equals -vE. -t equals -vT. -u is accepted but has no effect.\"\n      - id: R5\n        text: \"Implement error handling and exit codes per prd006-cat R5.1-R5.4: exit 0 when all inputs processed successfully. Exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened -- print error to stderr, continue processing remaining files. Exit 1 on stdout write error. Do not exit non-zero due to SIGPIPE.\"\n      - id: R6\n        text: \"Implement flag parsing: accept -n, -b, -s, -v, -E, -T, -A, -e, -t, -u as short flags including combined forms (e.g., -vET as a single argument). Accept '--' to terminate flag parsing. Report unrecognized flags to stderr and exit non-zero.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Apply transformations in the order specified by prd006-cat R4.9: squeeze blanks (-s), then non-printing display (-v/-T), then end-of-line marker (-E), then line number prefix (-n/-b). This order ensures squeezing happens on raw input, non-printing conversion does not interfere with numbering, and the dollar sign appears at the visual end of line.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use the same SIGPIPE handling pattern as cmd/ts/main.go: install a goroutine-based signal handler via signal.Notify that calls os.Exit(0) on syscall.SIGPIPE, per prd006-cat R5.4.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"When no transformation flags are active, use io.Copy for efficient binary passthrough per prd006-cat R1.4. When any transformation flag is active (-n, -b, -s, -v, -E, -T, or their combinations), process input byte-by-byte to apply the non-printing display logic correctly.\"\n      - id: D4\n        text: \"Use manual flag parsing (not the standard flag package) to support combined short flags like -vET matching GNU cat getopt behavior. Follow the same manual parsing pattern used in cmd/ts/main.go parseFlags function.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/cat compiles with 'go build ./cmd/cat/...' with no errors.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"printf 'hello\\\\nworld\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/cat produces 'hello\\\\nworld\\\\n' verbatim -- no added or removed bytes.\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"printf 'alpha\\\\n\\\\nbeta\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/cat -n produces three numbered lines: '     1\\\\talpha\\\\n     2\\\\t\\\\n     3\\\\tbeta\\\\n'.\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"printf 'first\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nsecond\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/cat -b produces numbered non-blank lines only: '     1\\\\tfirst\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n     2\\\\tsecond\\\\n'.\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"printf 'a\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nb\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/cat -s produces 'a\\\\n\\\\nb\\\\n' -- consecutive blanks collapsed to one.\"\n      - id: AC6\n        text: \"printf '\\\\x01\\\\x09\\\\x1b\\\\x7f\\\\x80\\\\xff' | go run ./cmd/cat -v produces '^A' for 0x01, literal tab for 0x09, '^[' for 0x1b, '^?' for 0x7f, 'M-^@' for 0x80, 'M-^?' for 0xff.\"\n      - id: AC7\n        text: \"printf '\\\\x01\\\\thello\\\\n' | go run ./cmd/cat -A produces '^A^Ihello$\\\\n' -- combined -vET behavior.\"\n      - id: AC8\n        text: \"go run ./cmd/cat nonexistent.txt realfile.txt prints error for nonexistent.txt to stderr, outputs content of realfile.txt to stdout, and exits 1.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":85993,"cache_read_input_tokens":18565,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":85993},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"89b02b81-8cd4-47f3-a924-f7655a560be9","uuid":"250f7aca-e4f9-4aa4-9199-53964e64726a"}
